The demodulation of a single sideband (SSB) signal involves coherent detection, which requires a local oscillator in phase with the carrier used during modulation. For a single tone message signal $ m(t) = \cos(\omega_m t) $, the SSB signal is typically of the form $ A \cos(\omega_c t \pm \omega_m t) $, where $ A $ is the carrier amplitude. 

When demodulating, the SSB signal is multiplied by the local oscillator $ \cos(\omega_c t) $, resulting in:
$$
A \cos(\omega_c t \pm \omega_m t) \cdot \cos(\omega_c t) = \frac{A}{2} \left[ \cos(2\omega_c t \pm \omega_m t) + \cos(\omega_m t) \right].
$$
After low-pass filtering, the high-frequency term $ \cos(2\omega_c t \pm \omega_m t) $ is removed, leaving:
$$
\frac{A}{2} \cos(\omega_m t).
$$
